======================================================================================================================
L12 -- A1 constraint-first dynamics: DOF count + the three open checks (foliation, matter, cosmology)
======================================================================================================================

SECTION 0 -- CONTROLS: does my ADM / determinant machinery reproduce general relativity?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    delta(ln det gamma) = tr(gamma^-1 delta gamma)  ->  delta q/delta gamma_ij = -(1/6) gamma^ij
    gamma_ij * (-(1/6) gamma^ij) = -1/2               ->  {q(x), pi(y)} = -(1/2) delta^3(x-y)
  [PASS] C0 [control] sympy reproduces the determinant variation and the trace identity that makes q conjugate to the momentum trace   (both exact)
    with pi^ij = (sqrt(gamma)/2kappa)(K gamma^ij - K^ij):  delta H[N]/delta pi^ij = -2 N K_ij
  [PASS] C1 [control] the ADM identity delta H[N]/delta pi^ij = -2 N K_ij holds for a general symmetric gamma and K   (exact, all 9 components)
  [PASS] C2 [control] flat 3-space has R = 0 in both Cartesian and spherical coordinates (curvature machinery)   (R_cart = 0, R_sph = 0)
    ADM GR: 2N = 12 (6 gamma_ij + 6 pi^ij), n_1st = 4 (H_perp, H_i), n_2nd = 0
    (2N - n_2nd - 2 n_1st)/2 = (12 - 0 - 8)/2 = 2
  [PASS] C3 [control] the counting rule (2N - n_2nd - 2 n_1st)/2 returns 2 for ADM general relativity   (the same rule is applied to A1 below)
    gamma_ij = (1 - 2 Phi/c^2) delta_ij  ->  q = Phi/c^2 + O(Phi^2)   [ok]
                                         ->  R^(3) = (4/c^2) lap Phi  [ok]
    static (K_ij = 0) Hamiltonian constraint R = 16 pi G rho / c^2  =>  lap Phi = 4 pi G rho.
  [PASS] C4 [control] the weak field gives q = Phi/c^2 and the ADM Hamiltonian constraint ALONE already yields the Newtonian Poisson equation   (both exact at O(Phi); this is the fact open check 2 turns on)

SECTION 1 -- THE CONSTRAINT AND ITS ALGEBRA
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    C_M = D_i[mu(y) D^i q] - S depends on gamma_ij (through q, through D_i, and through
    |Dq|^2 = gamma^ij d_i q d_j q) and on the matter fields inside S.  It contains NO gravitational
    momentum pi^ij.  Three brackets follow.

    (a) {C_M(x), C_M(y)} = 0 EXACTLY, since delta C_M / delta pi^ij = 0 identically.
    (b) delta gamma_ij / delta(lambda) under the flow generated by C_M[f] is delta C_M[f]/delta pi^ij = 0.
        A momentum-independent constraint moves the momenta and leaves the metric fixed.  It therefore
        cannot be the generator of normal hypersurface deformations (time evolution): whatever else it is,
        C_M cannot BE the Hamiltonian constraint of the theory.  A1 must be the branch in which C_M is
        imposed IN ADDITION to H_perp -- unless the unspecified source S carries pi (see section 6).
  [FAIL] Q1 [algebra] C_M generates a nonzero evolution of gamma_ij, so it can serve as the theory's Hamiltonian constraint (evolution generator)   (delta C_M/delta pi^ij = 0 identically => it generates zero metric evolution; C_M can only SUPPLEMENT H_perp, unless the source S is given pi-dependence (missing input, section 6))

    (c) {C_M[f], H_perp[N]} = INT (delta C_M[f]/delta gamma_ij)(delta H[N]/delta pi^ij)
                            = INT (delta C_M[f]/delta gamma_ij)(-2 N K_ij)          [control C1]
        -- EXACTLY proportional to the extrinsic curvature.  On time-symmetric vacuum data (K_ij = 0) the
        bracket vanishes identically, for every smearing f and every lapse N.  The claimed second-class
        PAIR does not exist there: the Dirac matrix degenerates and the count is not the generic one.
        The leading (two-derivative) piece is D_i[mu-hat^ij D_j (N K / 3)], from delta q = (1/3) N K, so the
        lapse-fixing equation d C_M/dt = 0 is elliptic with coefficient proportional to  mu * K.
        It degenerates twice over: at K -> 0 (quasi-static systems) and at mu -> 0 (deep-MOND field nulls).
        PRECISION, so this is not over-read: with matter present the bracket also picks up {S, H_matter},
        the flow of the source itself.  The exact statement is therefore -- the GRAVITATIONAL part of
        {C_M, H_perp} is exactly proportional to K_ij, and on time-symmetric VACUUM data (or a momentarily
        static matter configuration) the whole bracket vanishes identically.
  [FAIL] Q2 [algebra] the bracket {C_M, H_perp} that makes the pair second class is nonvanishing on time-symmetric vacuum data (K_ij = 0), so the second-class structure exists on the branch where MOND is applied   (the bracket is exactly proportional to K_ij and vanishes identically at K_ij = 0)

    (d) WHICH mode is removed.  Explicit test on gamma = diag(e^2A, e^2B, e^2C) with A,B,C functions of x
        (a background with |Dq| =/= 0, so mu =/= 0): vary A,B,C and collect every term in delta C_M carrying
        TWO derivatives of a variation.  If A1's claim is right they must all be proportional to
        (a+b+c)'' = the trace, i.e. the constraint removes the conformal/trace mode and leaves the two
        transverse-traceless polarisations.
        d(delta C_M)/d(a'') - d(delta C_M)/d(b'') = 0
        d(delta C_M)/d(b'') - d(delta C_M)/d(c'') = 0
        common coefficient on the background A=x, B=2x, C=-x/2 at x=0.2 with mu = 1 - e^-y : a'' -0.187556, b'' -0.187556, c'' -0.187556  (equal and nonzero)
  [PASS] Q3 [structure] every two-derivative term in the variation of C_M is proportional to the TRACE variation, so the mode C_M removes is the conformal mode and the survivors are the two tensor polarisations   (the three coefficients are equal and nonzero on a background with |Dq| =/= 0)

    (e) {C_M[f], H_i[xi]}.  H_i generates spatial diffeomorphisms, delta gamma_ij = D_i xi_j + D_j xi_i.
        A spatial SCALAR S obeys delta S = xi^k d_k S.  For q = -(1/6) ln det gamma:
            delta q = -(1/6) gamma^ij (L_xi gamma)_ij = xi^k d_k q - (1/3) d_k xi^k
        -- a scalar PLUS an anomaly.  det gamma is a density, not a scalar; its log is not a scalar.
        computed anomaly delta q - xi.dq = -Derivative(xi0(x, y, z), x)/3 - Derivative(xi1(x, y, z), y)/3 - Derivative(xi2(x, y, z), z)/3
        predicted  -(1/3) d_k xi^k      = -Derivative(xi0(x, y, z), x)/3 - Derivative(xi1(x, y, z), y)/3 - Derivative(xi2(x, y, z), z)/3    [match]
  [FAIL] Q4 [algebra] q transforms as a spatial scalar, so C_M is diffeomorphism-covariant and {C_M, H_i} closes on the constraint surface   (it does not: the anomaly -(1/3) d_k xi^k is nonzero for any volume-changing diffeomorphism, so C_M is second class with one combination of the momentum constraints)

    (f) THE COUNT.  Per space point: 2N = 12 (6 gamma_ij + 6 pi^ij).  Constraints H_perp, H_1, H_2, H_3, C_M.
        The GR block {H,H}, {H,H_i}, {H_i,H_j} closes weakly.  The only non-weakly-vanishing brackets are
        those in the C_M row/column: B = {C_M, H_perp} (prop. to K) and A_i = {C_M, H_i} (the anomaly).
        The 5x5 antisymmetric matrix therefore has one nonzero row and column -- rank 2 whenever (B, A_i)
        =/= 0.  So exactly ONE second-class pair, three surviving first-class combinations.
        generic branch (K =/= 0, anomaly present): rank = 2 -> n_2nd = 2, n_1st = 3 -> DOF = 2
        fiducial-repaired q, generic K:            rank = 2 -> DOF = 2
        time-symmetric branch K_ij = 0, repaired q: rank = 0 -> C_M first class -> n_1st = 5 -> DOF = 1
        time-symmetric branch, anomaly present:     rank = 2 -> DOF = 2
  [PASS] Q5 [count] on the generic branch the constraint matrix has rank 2, giving one second-class pair and (12 - 2 - 6)/2 = 2 gravitational degrees of freedom, as A1 claims   (the arithmetic is correct: 2 DOF, and Q3 shows they are the two tensor polarisations)
  [FAIL] Q6 [count] that count is uniform -- the constraint matrix keeps its rank on the branches A1 restricts itself away from, so the Dirac procedure is well defined across phase space   (it does not: once q is repaired into a scalar (F3, which covariance forces) the anomaly A_i vanishes, and on time-symmetric data B vanishes too, so the matrix drops to rank 0, C_M turns first class and the count falls to 1.  A rank-changing constraint system has no uniform Dirac bracket; 2 is the count on one side of that surface only)

SECTION 2 -- OPEN CHECK 1: FOLIATION.  Is q a property of the spacetime, or of the slicing?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    q is built from det gamma.  det gamma is the volume element OF A SLICE, in A COORDINATE SYSTEM.
    Two independent tests: (F1) the same spacetime cut two ways; (F2) the same slice in two coordinate
    systems.  Both use vacuum Schwarzschild / empty flat space, where the answer is unambiguous.

    F2 -- EMPTY FLAT SPACE, Cartesian vs spherical coordinates.
        Cartesian : det gamma = 1, q = 0, C_M = 0
        spherical : det gamma = r^4 sin^2(theta), q = -log(r**4*sin(theta)**2)/6
                    |Dq|^2 = (4 + tan(theta)**(-2))/(9*r**2);  |Dq| = 2/(3*r) on the equator
                    c^2|Dq|/a0 at 1 AU (equator) = 4.278e+15  => mu = 1 to machine precision
                    y = 1 only at r = 6.400e+26 m = 20.7 Gpc -- beyond the observable universe, so the artefact sits in the mu = 1 (Newtonian) regime everywhere
                    C_M = D_i[mu D^i q] = -1/(3*r**2)   (nonzero)
        with source S = 4 pi G rho/c^2, C_M = 0 demands a fictitious density
          at 1 AU   : rho = -1596 kg/m^3   (denser than granite, and negative)
          at 10 kpc : rho = -3.752e-16 kg/m^3   (~1e5 x the mean baryon density of a galaxy)
        Both a0 footings give the identical number here because mu = 1 in both.
  [FAIL] F2 [spatial covariance] C_M vanishes in empty flat space in every spatial coordinate system, as any physical field equation must   (Cartesian gives 0, spherical gives -1/(3*r**2) =/= 0; the same empty space demands rho = -1.6e+03 kg/m^3 at 1 AU.  q is a log-DENSITY, not a scalar (check Q4))

    F3 -- THE REPAIR, and what it costs.  Take q = -(1/6) ln(det gamma / det gamma-bar) with a
         fiducial FLAT gamma-bar (the BSSN conformal factor).  q is then a genuine scalar and F2's
         anomaly is gone.  But which flat gamma-bar?  Nothing in A1 fixes it.  Schwarzschild, one
         slice, two admissible flat fiducials:
         areal radius     : q = -log(-r/(2*M - r))/6  = -M/(3r) + O(M^2)
                            ->  c^2|Dq| = 2.99585059578939e+16*M/r**2 + O(M^2)   = (c^2/3) M/r^2 = g_Newton / 3
         isotropic radius : q = -M/rbar
                            ->  c^2|Dq| = 8.98755178736818e+16*M/rbar**2 + O(M^2)   = c^2 M/rbar^2 = g_Newton
         (M is the geometric mass GM/c^2; c^2 = 8.987552e+16, c^2/3 = 2.995851e+16)
  [FAIL] F3 [fiducial repair] with the fiducial repair the MOND field c^2|Dq| is unique -- independent of which flat fiducial is chosen   (it is not: the areal fiducial gives g_N/3 and the isotropic fiducial gives g_N, a factor 3.00000000000000 in the predicted MOND acceleration.  Nothing in A1 selects one)

    F1 -- THE SAME SPACETIME, TWO SLICINGS.  Vacuum Schwarzschild, static slicing vs
         Painleve-Gullstrand.  PG has gamma_ij EXACTLY FLAT and all the curvature in K_ij.
  [PASS] C5 [control] the Painleve-Gullstrand slice of Schwarzschild satisfies the VACUUM ADM Hamiltonian constraint R + K^2 - K_ij K^ij = 0 (my K_ij machinery is right)   (R = 0, K^2 - K_ijK^ij = 0, exact)
         static slicing (isotropic fiducial) : c^2|Dq| = 8.98755178736818e+16*M/rbar**2  = g_Newton
         Painleve-Gullstrand slicing         : det gamma = det gamma-bar exactly, q = 0, c^2|Dq| = 0
         Same spacetime.  Same mass.  Same matter (none).  The whole MOND field is a property of the cut.
  [FAIL] F1 [foliation] the MOND field c^2|Dq| built from det gamma is a property of the SPACETIME: two slicings of vacuum Schwarzschild give the same field   (they do not: the static slice gives g_Newton, the Painleve-Gullstrand slice of the SAME spacetime gives exactly 0.  A1 is therefore a statement about a preferred foliation, not about geometry)

    F4 -- WHAT FIXES THE FOLIATION, and how strongly.  Preservation d C_M/dt = 0 uses Q2's bracket:
         an elliptic equation for the lapse with principal part (1/3) D_i[mu K D^i N].  The lapse is
         DETERMINED, not free -- that IS the preferred foliation -- and the equation is elliptic, so
         N responds instantaneously across the slice.  Its coefficient is mu K.  Cosmologically
         K = -3H, so compare |K| with the local dynamical rate omega = v/R of the system it must control:
           Solar System, 1 AU      omega = 1.991e-07 s^-1   |K|/omega = 3.29e-11
           galaxy, 10 kpc          omega = 6.482e-16 s^-1   |K|/omega = 1.01e-02
           cluster, 1 Mpc          omega = 3.241e-17 s^-1   |K|/omega = 2.02e-01
  [FAIL] F4 [foliation/strong coupling] the lapse-fixing equation stays non-degenerate in the quasi-static regime where MOND is applied (criterion, stated: |K|/omega > 1e-3 in all three systems)   (the coefficient is proportional to K = -3H; |K|/omega = 3.3e-11 in the Solar System and 1.0e-02 in a galaxy -- the Solar-System value is eight orders below any threshold one could pick, so the verdict does not depend on the 1e-3 convention.  The second-class pairing that buys the count is nearly degenerate exactly where the theory must work (P7 pattern))

SECTION 3 -- OPEN CHECK 2: MATTER.  Couple S_m[g, psi] minimally (I2).  Does the constraint survive?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Minimally coupled matter conserves itself by construction: nabla_mu T^munu = 0 follows from the
    diffeomorphism invariance of S_m[g,psi] alone, and check Q4's anomaly lives in the GRAVITY sector.
    So the matter Ward identity is not where this breaks.  The break is COMPATIBILITY: control C4 showed
    that the ADM Hamiltonian constraint by itself already fixes the same function q that C_M fixes.

    Static weak field, both constraints imposed (A1's only available branch, per check Q1):
        H_perp = 0 :   lap q          = 4 pi G rho / c^2        (control C4)
        C_M    = 0 :   div[mu(y) grad q] = 4 pi G rho / c^2
    Subtract:          div[(1 - mu) grad q] = 0,   1 - mu = e^-y > 0 strictly.
    In spherical symmetry that integrates exactly: e^{-y} g r^2 = const = Cq, with g = c^2|dq/dr|.
    The function g -> g e^{-g/a0} has a maximum a0/e, so Cq/r^2 <= a0/e is required for ALL r:
        canonical : max_g [g e^(-g/a0)] = a0/e = 3.4441e-11 m/s^2 at g = a0 = 9.3619e-11
        alt       : max_g [g e^(-g/a0)] = a0/e = 4.1493e-11 m/s^2 at g = a0 = 1.1279e-10
        As r -> 0 near any mass this forces Cq = 0, hence g == 0 everywhere: imposing C_M on top of
        the ADM Hamiltonian constraint admits NO solution with a gravitational field at all.  (Equivalently,
        integrating q * div[e^{-y} grad q] over space with q -> 0 at infinity gives INT e^{-y}|grad q|^2 = 0.)

    Numerically, for M_b = 1e10 Msun at r = 10 kpc, the two constraints demand:
        canonical : H_perp wants g = 1.3942e-11 m/s^2 ; C_M wants g = 4.0053e-11 m/s^2 -> ratio 2.873
        alt       : H_perp wants g = 1.3942e-11 m/s^2 ; C_M wants g = 4.3537e-11 m/s^2 -> ratio 3.123
  [FAIL] M1 [matter] C_M is compatible with the ADM Hamiltonian constraint for a generic matter distribution, so the coupled system has solutions with matter in it   (it is not: the difference is div[e^-y grad q] = 0, which forces grad q == 0.  Numerically the two constraints demand accelerations differing by a factor 2.87 (canonical) / 3.12 (alt) at 10 kpc)
  [FAIL] M2 [matter] the source S in C_M is fixed by the construction rather than being free input   (it is not stated in A1.  If S = 4 pi G rho/c^2 the system is over-determined (M1).  If S carries extrinsic-curvature terms, C_M becomes a DEFORMED Hamiltonian constraint replacing H_perp -- a different theory, and exactly warning P3.  See section 6)

    The P3 branch, stated so it is not silently dismissed.  If C_M REPLACES H_perp (S carrying K-terms),
    M1 evaporates.  What replaces it is the Hojman-Kuchar-Teitelboim uniqueness theorem (1976): with the
    standard momentum constraint and an H_perp quadratic in the momenta, the hypersurface-deformation
    algebra has GR as its only solution (up to G and Lambda).  A mu(y)-deformed H_perp cannot close it, so
    foliation invariance is lost in that branch too -- which is the SAME verdict F1 reaches directly, by a
    different route.  This programme's own record reached it a third time, empirically: the MMG
    constraint-first chassis was withdrawn on 2026-08-27 for gamma_PPN = 0, alpha_1 = +4, alpha_3 = -1 and
    Newtonian-order matter non-conservation, with the note that deleting the Hamiltonian constraint is
    simultaneously what buys the 2-DOF count and what kills lensing and conservation (RETRACTIONS.md).
    This lane reproduces the structural half of that from the definitions, independently.
  [PASS] M3 [matter] matter conservation nabla_mu T^munu = 0 is intact for minimally coupled matter   (it is -- it follows from the diffeomorphism invariance of S_m[g,psi] alone and is untouched by anything in the gravity sector.  This one is a genuine PASS)
    SCOPE of that PASS, so it is not read as contradicting the historical record.  It is a statement
    about the MATTER sector only.  In the supplement branch the gravity side carries S_GR + INT lambda C_M,
    whose multiplier term is not diffeomorphism covariant (check Q4), so nabla_mu T^munu = 0 does not
    propagate into a consistent gravitational field equation -- it becomes an integrability condition on
    lambda and the geometry.  That is the same over-determination M1 exhibits, seen from the Ward-identity
    side.  In the OTHER branch, where H_perp is deleted outright, the effective source is the thing that
    fails to be conserved -- which is what the 2026-08-27 MMG withdrawal reports at Newtonian order.  Both
    records are consistent; they are statements about different objects.

SECTION 4 -- OPEN CHECK 3: COSMOLOGY.  Evaluate on FLRW.  Trivially satisfied, or forcing?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    gamma_ij = a(t)^2 delta_ij  ->  q = -log(a(t)**6)/6 = -ln a  (spatially constant)
    D_i q = [0, 0, 0]  ->  y = 0  ->  mu(0) = 1 - e^0 = 0  ->  D_i[mu D^i q] = 0
    So C_M = 0 - S = 0 forces S = 0 exactly.  With S = 4 pi G rho/c^2 that is rho = 0:
    the constraint is NOT trivially satisfied on FLRW -- it FORCES an empty universe.
    a0 does not appear anywhere in this statement: it holds on both footings identically.
  [FAIL] K1 [cosmology] there is an expanding FLRW solution with rho > 0   (not in the branch A1 leaves available (check Q1): D_i q = 0 on any homogeneous slice, so C_M reduces to -S = 0 and the physical density is forced to vanish.  Only the P3 branch (S carrying K-terms, i.e. C_M replacing H_perp) escapes, and that branch is the one F1/HKT close)

    Linear perturbations about ANY homogeneous background (|Dq| = 0 there, so mu = 0 there).
    Take a single mode q = eps cos(k x) on a flat slice and expand C_M in the amplitude eps:
        symbolic O(eps^1) coefficient = 0      (no term linear in the perturbation at all)
        numerical order at k = 1/(10 kpc): |C_M| = 3.2195e-55, 3.2199e-57, 3.2200e-59 for eps = 1e-10, 1e-11, 1e-12
        d log|C_M| / d log eps = 2.0000   (1 would be linear; 2 is the degenerate deep-MOND operator)
        mu(y) = 1 - e^-y ~ y = c^2|Dq|/a0 makes D_i[mu D^i q] quadratic in Dq -- a degenerate
        (3-Laplacian-type) operator.  At linear order the constraint therefore contains no metric
        perturbation at all and reduces to delta S = 0, i.e. delta rho = 0: no linear growth of
        structure, and the mode Q3 was supposed to remove is not removed on these backgrounds.
  [FAIL] K2 [cosmology] the linearised constraint about a homogeneous background constrains the METRIC perturbation (as Q3 requires), rather than the matter   (it does not: mu(0) = 0 makes the operator start at O(eps^2) (measured slope 2.0000), so at linear order C_M = -delta S and the constraint forces delta rho = 0.  Q3's mode removal fails on every background with |Dq| = 0)

SECTION 5 -- THE TWO PHENOMENOLOGY GATES.  Is this the framework's theory at all?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Gate 1: does the static weak-field limit give MOND with mu(y) = 1 - e^-y and the programme's a0?
    With q = Phi/c^2 (control C4) and S = 4 pi G rho/c^2, C_M = 0 reads exactly
        div[ mu(|grad Phi|/a0) grad Phi ] = 4 pi G rho          -- Milgrom's equation, the frozen kernel.
    Deep-MOND limit mu -> y: g^2/a0 = G M/r^2  ->  v^4 = G M a0.  Solved numerically below to confirm the
    limit is approached by the FULL kernel and not just asserted.
        canonical  a0 = 9.3619e-11: v_flat -> 105.58 km/s for 1e10 Msun; v/(GMa0)^1/4 - 1 = +0.0166 (30 kpc) -> +0.00048 (1000 kpc); BTFR M_b = A v^4 with A = 80.5 Msun/(km/s)^4
        alt        a0 = 1.1279e-10: v_flat -> 110.62 km/s for 1e10 Msun; v/(GMa0)^1/4 - 1 = +0.0151 (30 kpc) -> +0.00044 (1000 kpc); BTFR M_b = A v^4 with A = 66.8 Msun/(km/s)^4
  [PASS] P1 [phenomenology] the static weak-field limit of C_M = 0 is exactly Milgrom's equation with the frozen kernel mu = 1 - e^-y, and reproduces v^4 = G M a0 on both footings   (it does, to <1% at 1000 kpc on both footings -- but only in the conformally-flat spatial gauge (check F3: the areal fiducial gives g_N/3, which would move a0 by a factor 9))

    Gate 2 (ingredient I4): is the screening controlled by a LOCAL dynamical quantity?  y = c^2|Dq|/a0 is
    the local acceleration -- not a potential, not an environment label, not a velocity dispersion.  The
    Solar System is deep inside a galaxy whose own field is ~ a0, and must still be screened by its own
    local g:
        canonical  Earth  1.00 AU: g = 5.930e-03 m/s^2, y = 6.334e+07, 1 - mu = e^-y = < 1e-300
        canonical  Saturn 9.58 AU: g = 6.461e-05 m/s^2, y = 6.902e+05, 1 - mu = e^-y = < 1e-300
        canonical  Neptune 30.1 AU: g = 6.545e-06 m/s^2, y = 6.991e+04, 1 - mu = e^-y = < 1e-300
        canonical  the surrounding galactic field g ~ 2.0e-10 m/s^2 (y = 2.14) does NOT enter: y is built from the TOTAL local |Dq|, which the Sun dominates by 3.2e+05x at Saturn
        alt        Earth  1.00 AU: g = 5.930e-03 m/s^2, y = 5.258e+07, 1 - mu = e^-y = < 1e-300
        alt        Saturn 9.58 AU: g = 6.461e-05 m/s^2, y = 5.729e+05, 1 - mu = e^-y = < 1e-300
        alt        Neptune 30.1 AU: g = 6.545e-06 m/s^2, y = 5.803e+04, 1 - mu = e^-y = < 1e-300
        alt        the surrounding galactic field g ~ 2.0e-10 m/s^2 (y = 1.77) does NOT enter: y is built from the TOTAL local |Dq|, which the Sun dominates by 3.2e+05x at Saturn
  [PASS] P2 [I4] screening is controlled by a local dynamical quantity (the acceleration), and the Solar System is screened while sitting inside a MOND-strength galactic field   (y > 1e5 out to Neptune on both footings; 1 - mu = e^-y is unmeasurably small)

    Ingredient I5: the corrections are e^-y -- exponentially small, regular, with no 1/y anywhere in the
    construction.  C_M contains mu(y) and nothing else; no inverse power of y is used to repair any order.
  [PASS] P3 [I5] Newtonian recovery is by exponentially small corrections with no singular 1/y factor   (1 - mu = e^-y, entire in y; the construction never inverts y)

SECTION 6 -- WHAT IS NOT DETERMINED BY WHAT IS PUBLISHED
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    A1 is one sentence.  Four inputs it does not supply, stated precisely rather than guessed:
      (i)   THE SOURCE S.  Is it 4 pi G rho/c^2 alone, or does it contain extrinsic-curvature terms
            (K^2 - K_ij K^ij)?  This decides everything downstream: with the first, C_M can only supplement
            H_perp (check Q1) and is over-determined (M1) and empty on FLRW (K1); with the second, C_M is a
            deformed Hamiltonian constraint -- warning P3 -- and F1/HKT decide it instead.
      (ii)  THE FIDUCIAL.  det gamma is a density.  Without a fiducial density, C_M is not even
            coordinate-covariant (F2).  With one, its value depends on which fiducial (F3).
      (iii) THE SLICING CONDITION.  q is a slice quantity (F1).  A1 needs one; supplying one is supplying
            a khronon, which is what I3a exists to avoid.
      (iv)  THE ACTION.  A1 gives a constraint, not a Lagrangian.  Without it there is no lensing
            calculation (Phi vs Psi), no PPN, and no multiplier structure -- so gates G6/G7 cannot even be
            posed here.  This lane does not claim them either way.
    Everything above that could be settled WITHOUT these inputs was settled; nothing was assumed to fill them.

SECTION 7 -- VERDICT
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Degree-of-freedom count on A1's generic branch: (12 - 2 - 2*3)/2 = 2, and check Q3 shows
    the removed mode is the conformal one, so the survivors ARE the two tensor polarisations.
    A1's arithmetic is CORRECT.  What it costs is what the three open checks measure.
      OPEN CHECK 1 foliation : FAIL  (F1 Painleve-Gullstrand gives zero MOND field for the same
                                      spacetime; F2 flat space in spherical coordinates demands
                                      -1.6e+03 kg/m^3 of fictitious matter at 1 AU;
